  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 1833b97b4e8284e489c3555d5dace838, type: 3}
  m_Name: PHP_05_IF_ELSE
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::CodeEscape.Programming.QuestionData
  id: PHP_05_IF_ELSE
  language: 2
  topic: If / Else
  difficulty: 0
  questionType: 2
  questionText: Type the condition so the code outputs "Door open" when $code is
    42.
  code: '<?php

    $code = 42;

    if (____) { echo "Door open"; }

    ?>'
  answerFormat: 1
  choices: []
  correctChoiceIndex: 0
  monospaceChoices: 0
  acceptedAnswers:
  - $code == 42
  - $code === 42
  - 42 == $code
  - 42 === $code
  caseSensitive: 1
  referenceFill: 
  runTests: []
  explanation: The condition goes inside the round brackets. $code == 42 compares
    the variable with 42, so the code in the curly braces runs and outputs Door open.
    A single = would assign instead of compare.
  hint: Round brackets around the condition, curly braces around the code.
  wrongChoiceFeedback: []
  wrongAnswerFeedback: Compare the variable $code with 42. One = stores a value;
    comparing needs a different operator.
